# The edge VM's environment. Copy to `infrastructure/edge/.env` ON THE BOX and fill it in.
#
#   * This file is committed and carries no values for anything secret. The real file lives
#     only on the VM, is gitignored, and is never printed into a log, a CI job or a ticket.
#   * `preflight.sh` reads the key names from THIS file and refuses to deploy when the box's
#     `.env` is missing one, leaves it empty, or still carries a `replace-me` placeholder. So
#     adding a key here is what makes it required — do that first, deploy second.
#   * Values marked GENERATE FRESH must be generated on the box and must never be copied from
#     development, from another environment, or from a chat window. Reusing a development
#     value in production is the failure this file exists to prevent, and `platform_core`
#     independently refuses to start with a `dev-only-` or `ci-only-` value in production.
#
# Generate every secret below with:  openssl rand -hex 32
#
# ---- how this file is annotated (env-audit) ----------------------------------------------
# Every KEY= line is preceded by a `>> KEY` block: required / secret / source / format /
# generate / consumer / since. The full per-variable table, the parser evidence and the list of
# decisions still open are in docs/deploy/ENV_SOURCE_MATRIX.md; the manual deploy, in order, is
# docs/deploy/ENV_SETUP_CHECKLIST.md. (docs/deploy/env-reference.md is the older reference, superseded
# in part.)
#
#   source   EXTERNAL  a value only another service can give you (a provider, a dashboard)
#            GENERATE  a value you must create on the box (the command is given)
#            CHOOSE    a free, non-secret configuration choice (a default is committed)
#            DERIVED   computed from other values in this file
#            `source` says where a value comes from on a FRESH box. On the box that is already
#            running, some keys tagged GENERATE or CHOOSE (POSTGRES_PASSWORD, OPSGRIDS_DB_PASSWORD,
#            INTERNAL_SERVICE_TOKEN, PII_ENCRYPTION_KEY, RABBITMQ_PASSWORD, RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET, the
#            signing key file, EDGE_HOST) very probably already exist (inferred from the production ref's
#            example and preflight; not determinable from the repo): NEVER regenerate them. The
#            matrix's category D lists them.
#   required "required" = present AND non-empty; "optional-empty" = the LINE must exist but
#            the value may be blank (preflight lists the exceptions in `optional_empty`).
#
# Secrets. Every required secret below is EMPTY on purpose, exactly as on integration. Two independent
# checks refuse an empty one: preflight ("missing or empty in .env") and, for INTERNAL_SERVICE_TOKEN,
# PII_ENCRYPTION_KEY and ADMIN_GATEWAY_HOP_KEY, every service itself (an empty value falls back to the
# `dev-only-` default, which platform_core refuses in production). A visible `CHANGE_ME_...` placeholder
# would defeat the second check: it is long and does not start `dev-only-`, so with one in place auth,
# notification and payment all START in production on a repo-public key (measured against integration
# 5320848 with their real Settings classes; notification then had Engage link signing ON). So none is
# used. Optional secrets stay empty too. No real value of any kind belongs in this file - it is committed.
# The real one lives only on the box.
#
# This file describes the FINAL release: release/backlog-complete at 5320848 (ops-monitor,
# domain-verification, crm-leads, the staff console backend / MFA reseal, the Engage UI, the billing
# run, memory-headroom and io-outside-txn are all merged; io-outside-txn adds no key). Deploy a ref
# that contains it (`git merge-base --is-ancestor 5320848 <ref>`). The preflight fixes alone need
# ce35c77: on 193b529 or earlier, preflight would demand two keys it does not yet allow to be empty
# or absent (EDGE_PROFILES, EDGE_BUDGET_ACCEPTED_MB) and would fail on two known defects (matrix,
# section 11).
# Which heads were read: "Provenance" in docs/deploy/ENV_SOURCE_MATRIX.md (read 2026-09-24; it
# supersedes the older table in docs/deploy/env-reference.md). Regenerate when they move.
# The step-by-step for the manual deploy is docs/deploy/ENV_SETUP_CHECKLIST.md.
#
# `.env` is `source`d by bash (preflight.sh, backup.sh) AND parsed by docker compose, two different
# parsers. Rules that hold for both (each was run against preflight and compose, see the matrix):
#   * A plain value (letters, digits and . _ - / : @ , = +) needs no quotes. Hex from `openssl rand -hex` is one.
#   * A value with a SPACE or one of ; & | < > ( ) : wrap it in DOUBLE quotes.
#   * A value with `$`, a backtick or a backslash: wrap it in SINGLE quotes. Double quotes do NOT protect
#     them: bash expands $VAR, $(cmd) and backticks inside double quotes and docker compose expands $VAR
#     too. Preflight would RUN a `$(...)` inside double quotes, or abort with "unbound variable" and no
#     FAILED footer. A value that also holds a single quote: regenerate it.
#   * `#` starts a comment only after whitespace (`ab#cd` is literal, `ab #cd` is cut to `ab`).
#   * Close every quote. An unbalanced quote (or a bare quote inside a value) is NOT caught by preflight's
#     pre-check: preflight ends with a bash syntax error (exit 2, no FAILED footer).
# preflight.sh refuses, by name and before sourcing the file, an UNQUOTED value with whitespace or a shell
# metacharacter (; & | < > ( ) backtick $( ), text after a closing quote, and CRLF line endings. It does
# not look inside quoted values, does not flag a plain $VAR, and does not flag an unbalanced quote.
# Where a key appears twice the LAST line wins (preflight, compose, remote.sh's dotenv_get). Four keys are
# read the old way, from the FIRST line: OPSGRIDS_DB_PASSWORD and OPS_MONITOR_PORT (remote.sh) and
# EDGE_WEB_ROOT and PUBLIC_ASSET_BASE_URL (publish-web.sh, raw: no unquoting, no comment stripping): write
# those four bare, one line each. remote.sh's dotenv_get matches only lines that START with `KEY=` at
# column 0: write EDGE_HOST, EDGE_PROFILES, COMPOSE_PROFILES and EDGE_UP_PARALLEL_LIMIT as `KEY=value`,
# never `export KEY=value` or indented (preflight accepts both, remote.sh does not, so they would judge
# different profile lists). Keep every key exactly once.

# --- identity of this box -----------------------------------------------------
# What this box is. `make deploy CONFIRM=...` must match it exactly, so a deploy aimed at the
# wrong host fails before it changes anything instead of quietly succeeding.
# >> EDGE_HOST
#    required: required | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (this box's DNS name; committed default is
#              fine)
#    format:   bare hostname, no scheme or path. `make deploy CONFIRM=<this>` must equal it exactly
#              (remote.sh assert-host). PUBLIC_ASSET_BASE_URL below must be built from it.
#    consumer: caddy, storefront, ops-monitor (certificate probe SNI), preflight.sh, remote.sh |
#              since: main
EDGE_HOST=edge.opsgrids.com
# The reserved (floating) IPv4 attached to this VM, if the provider offers one. Part of the
# platform's public contract once set: apex merchants put it in their zone file, and it is the
# value the merchant UI prints. Optional — `storefront`'s `Settings.edge_reserved_ip` defaults
# to empty and the routing probe prints "the platform address" instead of a literal until this
# is filled in, which is the honest thing to show rather than a made-up example. Leave it empty
# on a box with no floating IP yet; fill it in with the box's real address before the first
# merchant apex record is created. Never a documentation-range address (192.0.2.0/24,
# 198.51.100.0/24, 203.0.113.0/24, RFC 5737) — `preflight.sh` warns if it is.
# >> EDGE_RESERVED_IP
#    required: optional-empty | secret: no | source: EXTERNAL (the VM's real reserved/floating IPv4
#              from the cloud provider)
#    format:   IPv4 dotted quad, or empty. An RFC 5737 documentation address (192.0.2.*,
#              198.51.100.*, 203.0.113.*) only WARNS in preflight.
#    consumer: storefront (edge_reserved_ip) | since: main
EDGE_RESERVED_IP=
# Where the buyer bundle releases live on the host. `make edge-web` writes
# $EDGE_WEB_ROOT/releases/<utc-timestamp>/ and swaps $EDGE_WEB_ROOT/current.
# >> EDGE_WEB_ROOT
#    required: required | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (host directory; default is fine)
#    format:   absolute host path. Buyer bundle releases live in `<path>/releases/<utc-ts>/` with a
#              `current` symlink swapped by `make edge-web`. publish-web.sh reads this line raw over ssh
#              (first line, no unquoting, no comment stripping): write it bare, no quotes, no inline comment.
#    consumer: caddy (bind mount), publish-web.sh, preflight.sh (bundle warning) | since: main
EDGE_WEB_ROOT=/srv/opsgrids/web

# --- TLS ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The ACME account contact. Not a secret. Let's Encrypt emails this address about expiry, and
# in slice 1 that email is the ONLY way a human finds out a renewal has been failing — nothing
# is paged. Use a real, monitored inbox, not an alias nobody reads.
# >> ACME_EMAIL
#    required: required, and must NOT contain `example.com` (preflight placeholder rule) | secret:
#              no | source: CHOOSE (a real inbox a human reads)
#    format:   email address. The committed value ops@example.com is a placeholder that preflight
#              REFUSES; replace it.
#    consumer: caddy (ACME account contact) | since: main
ACME_EMAIL=ops@example.com
# The platform's own hostnames this box may hold a certificate for, comma-separated and exact.
# Every certificate is issued on demand and gated by the storefront's tls-authorize endpoint;
# a store answers for its own names, but EDGE_HOST and any API name are nobody's store and
# are refused unless listed here. A name that is not here and not a published store gets no
# certificate — which is the safe direction to be wrong in.
# >> TLS_PLATFORM_HOSTS
#    required: required | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (every platform name in DNS; committed
#              default)
#    format:   comma-separated exact lowercase hostnames, no spaces, no wildcard. preflight FAILs for
#              any literal `https://<name> {` site block in caddy/Caddyfile (thirteen) that is not
#              listed here. It does NOT check the EDGE_HOST site (`https://{$EDGE_HOST} {`) or
#              api.opsgrids.com: keep edge.opsgrids.com and api.opsgrids.com in the list by hand
#              (RUNBOOK: every platform name in its DNS table). All fifteen committed names stay.
#              admin.opsgrids.com is here unconditionally, like every other named site block, even
#              though the `admin` compose profile is off by default (RUNBOOK, "Turning on the admin
#              console") — the Caddyfile site exists regardless of profile and 502s until it is on.
#    consumer: storefront (tls-authorize issuance gate), preflight.sh | since: main
TLS_PLATFORM_HOSTS=edge.opsgrids.com,api.opsgrids.com,app.opsgrids.com,ops.opsgrids.com,worker.opsgrids.com,people.opsgrids.com,revenue.opsgrids.com,pulse.opsgrids.com,projects.opsgrids.com,support.opsgrids.com,marketing.opsgrids.com,finance.opsgrids.com,it.opsgrids.com,store.opsgrids.com,engage.opsgrids.com,admin.opsgrids.com

# --- database -----------------------------------------------------------------
# GENERATE FRESH. The Postgres superuser (`opsgrids`). Used by backups and by psql sessions in
# the runbook; no service connects with it.
# >> POSTGRES_PASSWORD
#    required: required | secret: YES | source: GENERATE on a FRESH box only (on the running box it
#              already exists, inferred: NEVER regenerate it; ENV_SOURCE_MATRIX category D)
#    format:   any string; hex recommended. preflight checks presence and placeholders only.
#    generate: openssl rand -hex 32
#    consumer: postgres (superuser `opsgrids`; backup.sh and runbook psql sessions) | since: main
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
# GENERATE FRESH. Shared by every `opsgrids_*` role — the per-service application roles, the
# admin role migrations run as, the BYPASSRLS readers. One password, many usernames, because
# each service rewrites only the username of DATABASE_URL onto its own role. Use hex or
# alphanumerics only: this value is substituted into a URL, and `@` or `/` will break it.
# >> OPSGRIDS_DB_PASSWORD
#    required: required | secret: YES | source: GENERATE on a FRESH box only (on the running box it
#              already exists, inferred: NEVER regenerate it; ENV_SOURCE_MATRIX category D)
#    format:   hex or alphanumerics ONLY: it is substituted into a database URL, so `@ / : ?` break
#              it.
#    generate: openssl rand -hex 32
#    consumer: every service (x-service-env -> DATABASE_URL), postgres-init role passwords | since:
#              main
OPSGRIDS_DB_PASSWORD=

# --- auth ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# GENERATE FRESH, at least 32 bytes. Signs the internal service tokens every hop presents.
# A development value here is refused at startup, by design.
# >> INTERNAL_SERVICE_TOKEN
#    required: required | secret: YES | source: GENERATE on a FRESH box only (on the running box it
#              already exists, inferred: NEVER regenerate it; ENV_SOURCE_MATRIX category D)
#    format:   >= 32 UTF-8 bytes; must not start `dev-only-` or `ci-only-`; must differ from
#              ADMIN_GATEWAY_HOP_KEY (platform_core/config.py, services/auth/app/config.py). Every
#              service refuses to start in production otherwise; preflight checks the 32 bytes and
#              the inequality first (lib/secret-strength.sh).
#    generate: openssl rand -hex 32
#    consumer: every service (x-service-env) | since: main
INTERNAL_SERVICE_TOKEN=
# GENERATE FRESH, at least 32 bytes, DIFFERENT from INTERNAL_SERVICE_TOKEN above. Signs the
# admin-gateway -> auth hop header on every operator request admin-gateway proxies (BLOCKER 2
# of the 2026-09-24 security review) — defence in depth on top of the route table already
# refusing /api/v1/auth/operator at every gateway. Only admin-gateway and auth read it.
# >> ADMIN_GATEWAY_HOP_KEY
#    required: required (preflight refuses it empty; auth refuses to start without it in production)
#              | secret: YES | source: GENERATE on the box
#    format:   >= 32 UTF-8 bytes; must not start `dev-only-` or `ci-only-`; must NOT equal
#              INTERNAL_SERVICE_TOKEN. Preflight enforces presence, >= 32 bytes and inequality
#              (lib/secret-strength.sh); compose passes it as `${...:-}` on purpose. Used verbatim
#              as the HMAC-SHA256 key (not hex/base64-decoded), so 64 hex chars = 256 bits is the
#              right shape.
#    generate: openssl rand -hex 32   (a separate invocation from INTERNAL_SERVICE_TOKEN)
#    consumer: auth ONLY (compose.prod.yml auth block; admin-gateway, the signer, is not deployed
#              here) | since: release/backlog-complete (staff console backend, merged at 13c7181)
ADMIN_GATEWAY_HOP_KEY=
# GENERATE FRESH, at least 32 bytes. The passphrase every tenant's PII is sealed under.
# Losing it means losing the ability to read encrypted PII: back it up somewhere that is not
# this box, before there is any data. Rotating it means putting the old value in
# PII_DECRYPT_KEYS (comma-separated) and the new one here.
# >> PII_ENCRYPTION_KEY
#    required: required | secret: YES | source: GENERATE on the box, ONCE, before any data exists
#    format:   passphrase, >= 32 UTF-8 bytes (checked by preflight), no `dev-only-`/`ci-only-`
#              prefix; HKDF-SHA256 turns it into an AES-256-GCM key. Back it up OFF the box. NEVER
#              regenerate on a box that already holds sealed data. Rotating through PII_DECRYPT_KEYS
#              keeps old rows readable but BREAKS every blind-index lookup (platform_core/pii.py:184-190)
#              and has no RUNBOOK procedure: it is an owner decision, not a routine step.
#    generate: openssl rand -hex 32
#    consumer: every service (x-service-env); auth also seals MFA TOTP secrets with it | since: main
PII_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# >> PII_DECRYPT_KEYS
#    required: optional-empty | secret: YES | source: DERIVED (the previous PII_ENCRYPTION_KEY
#              value(s), only while rotating)
#    format:   comma-separated old passphrases; an entry must not contain a comma (hex never does).
#              Empty = no rotation in progress.
#    consumer: every service (x-service-env) | since: main
PII_DECRYPT_KEYS=
# The Ed25519 private key file on the HOST, mounted read-only into auth-service. Generate on
# the box with `openssl genpkey -algorithm ed25519 -out <path>`; never reuse the development
# key from `make devkeys`. Only auth-service holds it; everything else verifies via JWKS.
# >> JWT_PRIVATE_KEY_HOST_PATH
#    required: required; the FILE must exist | secret: path: no / FILE: YES | source: GENERATE the
#              file on a FRESH box only (on the running box it already exists, inferred: NEVER
#              regenerate it, every live token would stop verifying; ENV_SOURCE_MATRIX category D)
#    format:   absolute host path to an UNENCRYPTED PKCS8 Ed25519 PEM. preflight FAILs unless the
#              file exists with mode 400, 440, 600 or 640. The auth container's uid must be able to
#              read it (RUNBOOK 'Secrets': chown it to the uid `docker compose run --rm --no-deps -T
#              --entrypoint id auth -u` prints; preflight checks the mode only, not readability).
#    generate: openssl genpkey -algorithm ed25519 -out /srv/opsgrids/secrets/jwt-ed25519.pem &&
#              chmod 600 /srv/opsgrids/secrets/jwt-ed25519.pem
#    consumer: auth ONLY (read-only bind mount) - the only private signing key in the platform |
#              since: main
JWT_PRIVATE_KEY_HOST_PATH=/srv/opsgrids/secrets/jwt-ed25519.pem
# The `kid` published in JWKS and stamped on every token. Bump it when the key is rotated.
# >> JWT_KID
#    required: required (present and non-empty for preflight/compose) | secret: no | source: CHOOSE
#    format:   free string. NOTE: nothing reads it - auth derives the real `kid` from the public
#              key's thumbprint (services/auth/app/keys.py), so this value is inert today.
#    consumer: interpolated into every service's environment (x-service-env); no code path reads it
#              | since: main
JWT_KID=prod-1
# Must match what every service expects; changing it invalidates every live token.
# >> JWT_ISSUER
#    required: required | secret: no | source: CHOOSE
#    format:   free string used as the JWT `iss` claim (an https URL by convention); identical for
#              every service. Changing it invalidates every live token.
#    consumer: every service (x-service-env); auth stamps `iss` | since: main
JWT_ISSUER=https://auth.opsgrids.com

# --- broker -------------------------------------------------------------------
# GENERATE FRESH. RabbitMQ's application user. Every relay publishes and every consumer reads
# with it; it never leaves the compose network.
# >> RABBITMQ_PASSWORD
#    required: required | secret: YES | source: GENERATE on a FRESH box only (on the running box it
#              already exists, inferred: NEVER regenerate it; ENV_SOURCE_MATRIX category D)
#    format:   any string; hex recommended (it is embedded in the broker URL).
#    generate: openssl rand -hex 32
#    consumer: rabbitmq, every service's broker URL (x-service-env), ops-monitor DLQ probe | since:
#              main
RABBITMQ_PASSWORD=

# --- products this box sells (ADR-0013 §5) ------------------------------------
# Comma-separated compose profiles, one per sellable product: `inventory` (the operations
# service), `storefront`, and one named after each other product's catalogue key. Read by
# docker compose itself, and always explicit — write down every profile this box sells by hand
# (M4/review round 2: nothing computes this list for you any more; see below for why). Preflight
# refuses to deploy when the catalogue marks a product `available` whose profile is not listed
# here (a 504 waiting to happen), refuses outright when a product with a real activation has no
# profile running (a paying customer losing their product), and warns — never refuses — when a
# profile here has zero activations and is not even sold in the catalogue: safe to consider
# trimming, never forced.
#
# Left EMPTY here on purpose (the example used to turn every profile on at once — do not copy
# that back in: memory-budget.json's re-measured numbers show that combination no longer fits
# this box's hard budget line at all, let alone with margin for what is still on the backlog —
# see ADR-0013's memory-headroom addendum). Empty means core only, exactly and only that — an
# earlier version of this file computed a default from the catalogue's own activations when both
# this and EDGE_PROFILES were empty; that was removed (H2, review round 2) because `docker
# compose up` does not stop a container whose profile was silently removed, so the computed value
# never actually changed what ran, only what preflight/remote.sh *believed* was running. A human
# writes this list; nothing infers it any more.
#
# What the repo records about production (ADR-0013's memory-headroom addendum, from a read-only
# look at the box during review): its running `.env` lists all ten profiles and has no
# EDGE_PROFILES. Integration shipped exactly that string here until the memory-headroom merge (193b529):
#   inventory,storefront,hrms,crm,pulseboard,projects,support,marketing,finance,itsm
# Declaring all ten on the final release prices over the hard memory line (see
# EDGE_BUDGET_ACCEPTED_MB below) - an owner decision, not something this file makes for you.
# >> COMPOSE_PROFILES
#    required: optional-empty (empty = core only) | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (which products this
#              box sells)
#    format:   comma-separated lowercase compose profile names, NO spaces: inventory, storefront,
#              hrms, crm, pulseboard, projects, support, marketing, finance, itsm. A non-empty
#              EDGE_PROFILES REPLACES this (it does not add to it). preflight FAILS a name
#              compose.prod.yml does not define ("is not defined in compose.prod.yml, so it enables
#              nothing"); a malformed one (upper case, `;`, `.`, `-`) also stops ops-monitor at
#              startup (ops_monitor/config.py). `*` (every profile) is valid for docker compose and
#              ops-monitor, and preflight/remote.sh expand it to the ten names (on 193b529 they did
#              not). Write the list by hand.
#    consumer: docker compose (every call), preflight.sh, remote.sh, ops-monitor (derives its check
#              lists) | since: main (default became EMPTY with the memory-headroom merge, ce35c77;
#              before it integration shipped all ten)
COMPOSE_PROFILES=

# Optional (may be absent from an existing box's .env entirely, not just empty). An explicit,
# authoritative override for which product profiles run — set this instead of COMPOSE_PROFILES
# when a profile must run ahead of its first activation (onboarding a product, a demo). Always
# wins over COMPOSE_PROFILES in both preflight.sh and remote.sh. Same comma-separated profile
# names as COMPOSE_PROFILES above.
# >> EDGE_PROFILES
#    required: optional-empty, and the line may be ABSENT entirely (preflight optional_absent) |
#              secret: no | source: CHOOSE (an explicit override)
#    format:   same syntax as COMPOSE_PROFILES (comma-separated, no spaces). Empty = use
#              COMPOSE_PROFILES. preflight FAILS a name compose.prod.yml does not define; the last
#              EDGE_PROFILES= line in the file wins. (`COMPOSE_PROFILES=*`, every profile, is valid
#              for docker compose and preflight expands it, but a hand-written list is what this
#              file asks for.)
#    consumer: preflight.sh and remote.sh ONLY (exported to compose as COMPOSE_PROFILES); no
#              container reads it. A `docker compose ... --env-file .env` you run by hand does NOT see
#              it (compose reads only COMPOSE_PROFILES), so keep this empty or export COMPOSE_PROFILES
#              yourself for hand-run commands | since: memory-headroom, merged into
#              release/backlog-complete at ce35c77
EDGE_PROFILES=

# Optional (may be absent). The memory-headroom slice's own owner decision (B2, review round 2):
# production runs every product it sells, and the measured cost of that already exceeds this
# box's hard budget line before a single planned service is added (see ADR-0013's memory-headroom
# addendum for the numbers). Preflight refuses a deploy over the hard line UNLESS this is set and
# the deploy's own total is at or under it. **The value is a decision record: exactly the
# post-deploy total preflight printed for the commit being deployed**, written down only after
# reading it, as the record that the owner looked at the overcommit and chose to run at it anyway
# rather than resize the box or de-list a product. A number larger than that total is slack
# nobody decided on (preflight says so); above `host.max_accepted_percent` of MemTotal (125% in
# memory-budget.json) it is a typo and FAILS the deploy, and no ceiling covers a total above it.
# Never set this to make a refuse go away without reading why it refused first; clear it once the
# box has been resized or a product de-listed (preflight says when it is set but not needed).
#
# Kept UNCOMMENTED on purpose: tests/infra/test_preflight_memory.py builds its `.env` from this
# file's uncommented keys. It is optional-empty AND optional-absent in preflight on any ref that
# contains ce35c77 (before it, both keys were required). The number to write is the release's own,
# read off preflight on the box: on the merged tree the ten profiles price at 4581 MB against a
# 3862 MB box (docs/deploy/ENV_SOURCE_MATRIX.md, section 6.3), but the figure that counts is the one
# preflight prints at deploy time. Read the box first (RUNBOOK.md, 'How to read the box - a rule,
# not a feeling': docker stats and vmstat, scale 4581 x measured / 4044; above the cap, or swapping
# at a quiet hour, means resize or de-list instead of writing a ceiling).
# >> EDGE_BUDGET_ACCEPTED_MB
#    required: optional (empty/absent = no ceiling accepted) | secret: no | source: OWNER DECISION,
#              read off preflight's own printed projection
#    format:   whole number of megabytes, digits only: no unit, no decimal, no comma, no sign, no
#              leading zero (`04581` and `00` FAIL; a bare `0` is accepted and useless; bash drops an
#              unquoted trailing space or ` # comment`). Same unit as preflight's `<n>M` (sum of
#              memory-budget.json expected_rss_mb vs MemTotal kB / 1024, i.e. MiB in practice).
#              Non-digits FAIL preflight; above 125% of MemTotal (4827M on a 3862M box) FAILs; only
#              ever rescues an over-hard-line `refuse` verdict, and only a total at or below it.
#    consumer: preflight.sh ONLY; no container reads it | since: memory-headroom, merged into
#              release/backlog-complete at ce35c77
EDGE_BUDGET_ACCEPTED_MB=

# Optional, and deliberately COMMENTED OUT (an uncommented key here becomes required on every
# box). How many containers `docker compose up` starts at once during a deploy; four if unset.
# The deploy that merges a batch of new containers onto a box that is already swapping should
# set it to 2 for that one deploy (RUNBOOK.md, 'the memory budget'), then remove the line.
# >> EDGE_UP_PARALLEL_LIMIT  (COMMENTED ON PURPOSE: not part of the required key set)
#    required: optional; absent = 4 | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (a temporary throttle)
#    format:   a positive whole number; anything else (empty, 0, `2x`) silently falls back to 4.
#              remote.sh reads it from the process environment or from a `.env` line (the last
#              assignment wins; quotes, an inline ` # comment` and spaces are handled), because
#              `make deploy` runs over ssh and carries no shell environment. `up` prints the limit
#              it used.
#    consumer: remote.sh `up` ONLY (COMPOSE_PARALLEL_LIMIT) | since: memory-headroom (ce35c77)
# EDGE_UP_PARALLEL_LIMIT=2

# Optional, COMMENTED OUT for the same reason. The operations reconcile sweep, storefront's
# custom-domain sweep and document's purge sweep run inside their own API containers, and
# compose.prod.yml defaults this to `true`. Any other value STOPS all three — the `*-cron`
# containers that used to carry them no longer exist — and preflight warns when it is not `true`.
# >> RUN_SWEEPS_IN_API  (COMMENTED ON PURPOSE: not part of the required key set)
#    required: optional; absent = `true` (compose default) | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (leave it)
#    format:   a pydantic bool: `true`, `1`, `yes`, `on`, `y`, `t` (any case) keep the sweeps on; a
#              falsy value stops them; anything else is refused by the service at startup. preflight
#              reads it the same way and warns when it is off.
#    consumer: operations, storefront, document | since: memory-headroom (ce35c77)
# RUN_SWEEPS_IN_API=true

# Optional, COMMENTED OUT for the same reason. Every service that used to have its own
# `*-worker` container (account, product, subscription, payment, notification, approval,
# attendance, leave, customer, finance) now runs that worker's consumers, dispatchers, sweeps and
# drivers inside its own API container, and compose.prod.yml defaults this to `true`. Any other
# value STOPS all of them — the ten `*-worker` containers still exist in compose.prod.yml, but
# only start behind the `split-workers` profile, which is the rollback: set this to `false` and
# add `split-workers` to `EDGE_PROFILES` together.
# >> RUN_CONSUMERS_IN_API  (COMMENTED ON PURPOSE: not part of the required key set)
#    required: optional; absent = `true` (compose default) | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (leave it)
#    format:   a pydantic bool: `true`, `1`, `yes`, `on`, `y`, `t` (any case) keep the workers
#              folded in; a falsy value stops them — pair it with `split-workers` in
#              `EDGE_PROFILES` or event processing silently stops for every one of these services.
#    consumer: account, product, subscription, payment, notification, approval, attendance,
#              leave, customer, finance | since: worker-folding follow-up (2026-09-24)
# RUN_CONSUMERS_IN_API=true

# --- email --------------------------------------------------------------------
# `log` renders and records every message and sends nothing; `smtp` sends. A signup cannot
# set a password and a trial cannot be warned until this is `smtp` with working credentials.
# The @opsgrids.com mailboxes are Google Workspace: the platform sends as one of them through
# Google's submission server (STARTTLS on 587). SMTP_PASSWORD is an app password for that
# mailbox (Google account → Security → 2-Step Verification → App passwords), never the
# mailbox's own password. Google's SPF/DKIM/DMARC records for the domain live at the registrar.
# >> EMAIL_TRANSPORT
#    required: required | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (`smtp` once SMTP credentials exist)
#    format:   exactly `log` or `smtp` (case-sensitive). Any other value crash-loops
#              notification-worker; preflight does NOT validate it (it only warns on `log`). `log`
#              silences notification ONLY: ops-monitor ignores this key and sends whenever SMTP_HOST,
#              EMAIL_FROM and OPS_ALERT_EMAIL are all non-empty.
#    consumer: notification, notification-worker | since: main
EMAIL_TRANSPORT=log
# >> EMAIL_FROM
#    required: required | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (an address the SMTP account may send as)
#    format:   email address, used as the From header of platform mail; no format validation in
#              code.
#    consumer: notification, notification-worker, ops-monitor | since: main
EMAIL_FROM=info@opsgrids.com
# >> SMTP_HOST
#    required: optional-empty (but see format) | secret: no | source: EXTERNAL (the mail provider)
#    format:   hostname. An EMPTY value is treated as unset and notification silently falls back to
#              `localhost` - never leave it empty when EMAIL_TRANSPORT=smtp.
#    consumer: notification, notification-worker, ops-monitor | since: main
SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
# >> SMTP_PORT
#    required: required | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (the port that matches the provider's
#              submission service; the host, login and password are the EXTERNAL part)
#    format:   integer. 587 with SMTP_USE_TLS=true (STARTTLS). 465 (implicit TLS) works for
#              ops-monitor only, with SMTP_USE_SSL=true and SMTP_USE_TLS=false.
#    consumer: notification, notification-worker, ops-monitor | since: main
SMTP_PORT=587
# >> SMTP_USERNAME
#    required: optional-empty (needed by any relay that authenticates, Gmail does) | secret: no |
#              source: EXTERNAL (the mailbox that owns the app password)
#    format:   string, normally the full mailbox address.
#    consumer: notification, notification-worker, ops-monitor | since: main
SMTP_USERNAME=info@opsgrids.com
# >> SMTP_PASSWORD
#    required: optional-empty (needed with SMTP_USERNAME) | secret: YES | source: EXTERNAL (Google
#              Workspace app password for that mailbox; not generated here)
#    format:   opaque string. With spaces: wrap it in DOUBLE quotes. With `$`, a backtick or a
#              backslash: wrap it in SINGLE quotes (quoting rules at the top of this file; a value that
#              also holds a single quote: regenerate it). With a username set and no password every
#              ops-monitor alert send fails.
#    consumer: notification, notification-worker, ops-monitor | since: main
SMTP_PASSWORD=
# >> SMTP_USE_TLS
#    required: required | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (dictated by the provider's port)
#    format:   boolean (true/false; pydantic also accepts 1/0/yes/no/on/off). STARTTLS on the plain
#              port. Mutually exclusive with SMTP_USE_SSL. One shared key for notification AND
#              ops-monitor. With `false`, notification still logs in over a plain connection
#              (transports.py); only ops-monitor refuses to. Notification's `starttls()` passes no TLS
#              context (services/notification/app/transports.py:245-251), and the stdlib default context
#              verifies neither the certificate nor the host name (checked on Python 3.12): an on-path
#              attacker could read the app password. ops-monitor's own STARTTLS does verify.
#    consumer: notification, notification-worker, ops-monitor | since: main
SMTP_USE_TLS=true
# ops-monitor only (notification-service ignores it): implicit TLS from the first byte
# ("SMTPS", conventionally port 465) instead of STARTTLS on the plain port — mutually
# exclusive with SMTP_USE_TLS above; ops-monitor's mailer refuses both at once. Google's
# submission server works with the STARTTLS default; this exists for a provider that only
# offers SMTPS.
# >> SMTP_USE_SSL
#    required: optional-empty (line must exist) | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (true only for an
#              SMTPS-only provider)
#    format:   boolean. Implicit TLS (conventionally port 465). Never true together with
#              SMTP_USE_TLS=true: the mailer raises at SEND time (not at startup, not in preflight).
#    consumer: ops-monitor ONLY (notification has no implicit-TLS path) | since:
#              release/backlog-complete (ops-monitor, merged at 50756f7)
SMTP_USE_SSL=false

# --- payments -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Razorpay and Cashfree (ADR-0032). The key pairs may stay empty: the payment service then runs on
# its in-process stub and nothing reaches the network. **Keys alone never switch a network call
# on**: the wire is the stub unless PAYMENT_WIRE=live (Razorpay) or CASHFREE_ENV=sandbox|production
# (Cashfree) says otherwise, so a key pair can sit in this file safely until the owner decides.
# GENERATE FRESH the webhook secret regardless — production refuses the development default — and
# paste the same value into Razorpay's webhook settings (once a webhook path is made public; until
# then payment learns outcomes by polling the provider).
# >> RAZORPAY_KEY_ID
#    required: optional-empty (empty = the stub) | secret: no (public by design: Checkout is opened
#              in the browser with it) | source: EXTERNAL (Razorpay dashboard)
#    format:   a Razorpay key id: `rzp_test_...` or `rzp_live_...` (6-40 letters and digits after
#              the prefix). Set together with RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET or not at all: a half-set pair
#              stops `payment` starting. Used only when PAYMENT_WIRE=live; a `rzp_live_` key is
#              refused unless PAYMENT_ALLOW_LIVE_KEYS=true, and `rzp_test_` keys are refused on a
#              live wire on this (production) box unless PAYMENT_ALLOW_TEST_KEYS=true (a staging
#              box with no real paying customers only; see that key below).
#    consumer: payment, payment-worker | since: main (wired live by ADR-0032)
RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=
# >> RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET
#    required: optional-empty (set together with the key id) | secret: YES | source: EXTERNAL
#              (Razorpay dashboard)
#    format:   Razorpay key secret string. Authenticates the wire (HTTP basic) and verifies the
#              HMAC on a customer's checkout callback; never logged, never in an exception.
#    consumer: payment, payment-worker | since: main (wired live by ADR-0032)
RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET=
# >> RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET
#    required: required | secret: YES | source: GENERATE, then paste the same value into Razorpay's
#              webhook settings. The key is in the production ref's example and that ref's preflight
#              required it non-empty, so a value very probably already exists on this box (inferred, not
#              determinable from the repo): keep it; rotate only together with Razorpay (an owner decision)
#    format:   any string (HMAC-SHA256 key over the raw webhook body). PREFLIGHT refuses the
#              development default (`dev-only-*`, or a missing/empty line) first; behind it the
#              payment service itself refuses to START in production/staging/uat with an empty or
#              `dev-only-`/`ci-only-` value (an empty one falls back to the public development
#              default). No length floor in code; 64 hex chars recommended.
#    generate: openssl rand -hex 32
#    consumer: payment, payment-worker | since: main
RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# The provider switches and Cashfree's credentials below are COMMENTED OUT ON PURPOSE (an
# uncommented key here becomes required on every box, and preflight would refuse a deploy until it
# was filled in; the Maps key above has the same shape and the same reason). Absent or empty is the
# safe, supported state: new charges go to Razorpay, both wires are the stub, nothing moves real
# money. Turn a provider on with RUNBOOK.md, 'Payments: turning a provider on'; preflight
# (lib/payment-switches.sh) refuses every combination `payment` would refuse at start-up.
# >> PAYMENT_PROVIDER  (COMMENTED ON PURPOSE: not part of the required key set)
#    required: optional (default `razorpay`) | secret: no | source: OWNER DECISION
#    format:   `razorpay` or `cashfree`. The provider NEW platform charges and new checkouts go to.
#              An existing charge, mandate and webhook keep the provider they were created under:
#              flipping this is not a migration and flipping it back is the rollback. `cashfree`
#              on this (production) box needs CASHFREE_ENV=production (or, on a staging box only,
#              CASHFREE_ENV=sandbox with PAYMENT_ALLOW_TEST_KEYS=true).
#    consumer: payment, payment-worker | since: ADR-0032
# PAYMENT_PROVIDER=razorpay
# >> PAYMENT_WIRE  (COMMENTED ON PURPOSE: not part of the required key set)
#    required: optional (default `stub`) | secret: no | source: OWNER DECISION
#    format:   `stub` — Razorpay platform billing never opens a socket, whatever keys are present.
#              `live` — customer-present checkout against Razorpay (Phase A: there is no saved-
#              mandate debit); needs RAZORPAY_KEY_ID and RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET. Rollback: `stub`.
#    consumer: payment, payment-worker | since: ADR-0032
# PAYMENT_WIRE=stub
# >> PAYMENT_ALLOW_LIVE_KEYS  (COMMENTED ON PURPOSE: not part of the required key set)
#    required: optional (default `false`) | secret: no | source: OWNER DECISION
#    format:   `true` lets `rzp_live_...` keys and CASHFREE_ENV=production start. `false` refuses
#              every live-shaped key, on any switch. The platform's own counterpart of
#              COLLECTIONS_ALLOW_LIVE_KEYS (which is for tenants' keys).
#    consumer: payment, payment-worker | since: ADR-0032
# PAYMENT_ALLOW_LIVE_KEYS=false
# >> PAYMENT_ALLOW_TEST_KEYS  (COMMENTED ON PURPOSE: not part of the required key set)
#    required: optional (default `false`; never required) | secret: no | source: OWNER DECISION,
#              STAGING BOXES ONLY
#    format:   `true` lets TEST credentials run although this box says ENVIRONMENT=production:
#              `rzp_test_...` keys with PAYMENT_WIRE=live, and CASHFREE_ENV=sandbox (as the
#              default provider too). Only for a box with NO REAL PAYING CUSTOMERS: the providers'
#              public test cards and sandbox VPAs then pay invoices and no real money moves.
#              Refused beside PAYMENT_ALLOW_LIVE_KEYS=true, any live-shaped key or
#              CASHFREE_ENV=production (a box runs on test keys or on live money, never both).
#              While it is on, `payment` logs a WARNING at start-up (`payment.test_mode.enabled`),
#              preflight warns, and every checkout answers `test_mode: true`. Turn it off by
#              deleting the line (or `false`) together with the test keys: RUNBOOK.md, 'Payments:
#              test mode on a staging box'.
#    consumer: payment, payment-worker | since: ADR-0032 (staging test mode, 2026-10-06)
# PAYMENT_ALLOW_TEST_KEYS=false
# >> CASHFREE_ENV  (COMMENTED ON PURPOSE: not part of the required key set)
#    required: optional (default `stub`) | secret: no | source: OWNER DECISION
#    format:   `stub` — an in-process stateful fake, no network, whatever keys are present.
#              `sandbox` — Cashfree's test environment; REFUSED on this production box (public
#              test cards would pay real invoices for free), so it is for a local run — or for a
#              staging box with no paying customers, under PAYMENT_ALLOW_TEST_KEYS=true.
#              `production` — Cashfree's live API; needs both credentials and
#              PAYMENT_ALLOW_LIVE_KEYS=true.
#    consumer: payment, payment-worker | since: ADR-0032
# CASHFREE_ENV=stub
# >> CASHFREE_APP_ID  (COMMENTED ON PURPOSE: not part of the required key set)
#    required: optional-empty (set together with CASHFREE_SECRET_KEY) | secret: YES (held as a
#              SecretStr; never logged) | source: EXTERNAL (Cashfree dashboard, Payment Gateway
#              keys)
#    format:   Cashfree's App ID (`x-client-id`). A sandbox App ID starts with `TEST`.
#    consumer: payment, payment-worker | since: ADR-0032
# CASHFREE_APP_ID=
# >> CASHFREE_SECRET_KEY  (COMMENTED ON PURPOSE: not part of the required key set)
#    required: optional-empty (set together with CASHFREE_APP_ID) | secret: YES | source: EXTERNAL
#              (Cashfree dashboard)
#    format:   Cashfree's Secret Key (`cfsk_ma_test_...` / `cfsk_ma_prod_...`). It also SIGNS
#              Cashfree's webhooks (there is no separate webhook secret), so rotating it is a
#              flag day for the API calls and the deliveries together. A production-shaped key is
#              refused unless PAYMENT_ALLOW_LIVE_KEYS=true.
#    consumer: payment, payment-worker | since: ADR-0032
# CASHFREE_SECRET_KEY=

# --- tenant payment collection (ADR-0024 A4) ------------------------------------
# Each tenant connects its OWN Razorpay account in Operations → Payment settings; its key secret
# and webhook secret are sealed in payment's database, never in this file. These two lines are
# the platform owner's switches over whether any of that reaches Razorpay.
# >> COLLECTIONS_WIRE
#    required: optional (default `stub`) | secret: no | source: OWNER DECISION
#    format:   `stub` — no tenant call leaves the box; a checkout cannot be paid for real (the UI
#              says so). `razorpay` — a tenant's adapter calls api.razorpay.com over HTTPS with
#              that tenant's own keys (orders, payment status, refunds). Turn on only after a
#              sandbox (test-mode) run-through.
#    consumer: payment, payment-worker | since: feat/fulfilment-f10-online-payments
COLLECTIONS_WIRE=stub
# >> COLLECTIONS_CASHFREE_WIRE
#    required: optional (default `stub`) | secret: no | source: OWNER DECISION (ADR-0033)
#    format:   `stub` — a tenant's Cashfree account runs on the in-process fake; no call leaves the
#              box and a checkout cannot be paid for real (the UI says so). `live` — a tenant's
#              adapter calls Cashfree over HTTPS with that tenant's own App ID and Secret Key, at
#              sandbox.cashfree.com or api.cashfree.com as the tenant chose when connecting.
#              Turn on only after an owner-run sandbox run-through; Production accounts also need
#              COLLECTIONS_ALLOW_LIVE_KEYS=true.
#    consumer: payment, payment-worker | since: feat/merchant-cashfree
#    COMMENTED ON PURPOSE: preflight makes every uncommented key here required, and compose already
#    defaults it to `stub`, so a box without the line deploys (an uncommented line refused the
#    first deploy of a box that predates the key).
# COLLECTIONS_CASHFREE_WIRE=stub
# >> COLLECTIONS_ALLOW_LIVE_KEYS
#    required: optional (default `false`) | secret: no | source: OWNER DECISION
#    format:   `true` lets a tenant connect live keys (`rzp_live_…`, or a Cashfree account in
#              Production), which move real money. `false` accepts test-mode keys (`rzp_test_…`)
#              and Cashfree Sandbox only.
#    consumer: payment, payment-worker | since: feat/fulfilment-f10-online-payments
COLLECTIONS_ALLOW_LIVE_KEYS=false

# --- billing run tax (ADR-0005 addendum, 2026-09-23) --------------------------
# Which billing.tax_rates row the billing run applies to every invoice, e.g. gst / IN. Leave
# both empty until the deployment is registered for tax and a rate row exists: empty means no
# tax line. Set without a rate in force, the run refuses to invoice (and /health/billing-run's
# `failing` rises) rather than issuing an untaxed invoice.
# >> BILLING_TAX_CODE
#    required: optional-empty (line must exist) | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (only once the
#              deployment is tax-registered)
#    format:   the `code` of an existing billing.tax_rates row, e.g. gst. Set without a rate row in
#              force and the run refuses to invoice; no migration seeds a rate row, so insert it
#              (a new row, never an edit) BEFORE setting the code. Empty = no tax line.
#    consumer: billing | since: release/backlog-complete (billing run, merged at 193b529)
BILLING_TAX_CODE=
# >> BILLING_TAX_JURISDICTION
#    required: optional-empty (line must exist) | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (set together with
#              BILLING_TAX_CODE)
#    format:   free string, e.g. IN; optional. It is matched EXACTLY against the tax_rates row's
#              jurisdiction: empty matches only a row whose jurisdiction is NULL, so set it to the
#              same value the rate row carries (selectors.effective_tax_rate). Empty together with
#              an empty BILLING_TAX_CODE = no tax line.
#    consumer: billing | since: release/backlog-complete (billing run, merged at 193b529)
BILLING_TAX_JURISDICTION=

# The billing run itself (trial conversion, renewals, prorations, closing usage) is OFF unless
# this says `true`. Read `python -m app.billing_run --dry-run` first (the runbook's "The billing
# run"), then uncomment it. Left unset, preflight warns and the run stays off.
# >> BILLING_RUN_ENABLED  (COMMENTED ON PURPOSE: not part of the required key set)
#    required: optional; absent = OFF (preflight only WARNS) | secret: no | source: OWNER DECISION,
#              after `python -m app.billing_run --dry-run`
#    format:   write exactly `true` to turn it on. Preflight treats only the literal `true` as on
#              (anything else prints the OFF warning and skips the cutover check), but the worker
#              parses a pydantic boolean, so `1`, `yes` and `on` would also switch it ON in the
#              container, and a value with a stray space or quote inside it stops the worker
#              from starting. Compose defaults it to false. Turning it on converts every expired
#              trial and bills every overdue period.
#    consumer: subscription-worker | since: release/backlog-complete (billing run, merged at 193b529)
# BILLING_RUN_ENABLED=true
# Required with it: the cutover, the moment billing goes live, with a UTC offset. No recurring
# charge for a period that began before it is ever invoiced (existing subscriptions start paying
# at their first period after it; a trial that ended before it converts free) and no usage from
# before it is billed. Set it once, to the moment you turn the run on, and never move it later.
# >> BILLING_RUN_NOT_BEFORE  (COMMENTED ON PURPOSE: required ONLY when BILLING_RUN_ENABLED=true)
#    required: conditional; preflight FAILs if the run is enabled and this is empty or malformed |
#              secret: no | source: OWNER DECISION (the moment billing goes live)
#    format:   exactly YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM[:SS[.f]] then `Z` or `+HH:MM`/`-HH:MM` (services/subscription
#              CUTOVER_PATTERN; preflight uses the same pattern and also asks GNU `date` whether it
#              is a real instant, so 2026-09-31 FAILs). A naive time, a space instead of `T`,
#              `+0530` or epoch seconds are all refused; the service refuses them softly (the
#              billing run does not start, the rest of the worker keeps running). Preflight is a
#              necessary, not a sufficient, check: an offset of exactly +/-24:00 passes preflight (GNU
#              `date` accepts it) but the worker refuses it (datetime.fromisoformat), so the run silently
#              does not start: use a normal offset (+05:30) or Z, and confirm the worker logs
#              billing_run.cutover with the value it started with. Set once; never move it later.
#    consumer: subscription-worker | since: release/backlog-complete (billing run, merged at 193b529)
# BILLING_RUN_NOT_BEFORE=2026-10-01T09:00:00+05:30

# --- WhatsApp (notification's channel; ADR-0013 §2 makes it an add-on) ----------
# All optional until a Meta app exists. `log` sends nothing.
# >> WHATSAPP_TRANSPORT
#    required: required | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (`log` until a Meta app exists)
#    format:   exactly `log` or `whatsapp_cloud`. `whatsapp_cloud` without PHONE_NUMBER_ID and
#              ACCESS_TOKEN refuses to start notification-worker.
#    consumer: notification-worker | since: main
WHATSAPP_TRANSPORT=log
# >> WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET
#    required: optional-empty (needed to accept inbound webhooks) | secret: YES | source: EXTERNAL
#              (the platform's Meta app)
#    format:   string; HMAC key over the raw webhook body (X-Hub-Signature-256). Meta-issued (the
#              app's "App secret"). Platform-wide: one Meta app signs every tenant's webhooks. With
#              none configured the service REFUSES webhooks. Whether Meta's POST can reach
#              notification through Caddy and app-gateway on this box is not established: see
#              docs/deploy/ENV_SOURCE_MATRIX.md, section 9.
#    consumer: notification, notification-worker | since: main
WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET=
# >> WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN
#    required: optional-empty (needed for Meta's subscription handshake) | secret: YES | source:
#              GENERATE (you choose it, then paste it into the Meta app's webhook settings)
#    format:   non-empty ASCII string (compared with hmac.compare_digest as str).
#    generate: openssl rand -hex 32
#    consumer: notification, notification-worker | since: main
WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN=
# >> META_APP_ID
#    required: optional-empty (needed for the WhatsApp connect wizard) | secret: no | source:
#              EXTERNAL (the platform's Meta app: App ID)
#    format:   numeric string id; no validation. With any of the three META_* values missing the
#              wizard's `signup/config` answers `not_configured`.
#    consumer: notification, notification-worker | since: main
META_APP_ID=
# >> META_APP_SECRET
#    required: optional-empty (falls back to WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET when empty) | secret: YES |
#              source: EXTERNAL (the Meta app's "App secret")
#    format:   string; used for the Embedded Signup token exchange. It is the same Meta app
#              secret as WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET, so either may carry it.
#    consumer: notification, notification-worker | since: main
META_APP_SECRET=
# >> META_EMBEDDED_SIGNUP_CONFIG_ID
#    required: optional-empty (needed for the WhatsApp connect wizard) | secret: no | source:
#              EXTERNAL (Meta app > Facebook Login for Business > configuration)
#    format:   numeric string id; no validation.
#    consumer: notification, notification-worker | since: main
META_EMBEDDED_SIGNUP_CONFIG_ID=
# >> WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID
#    required: optional-empty (required iff WHATSAPP_TRANSPORT=whatsapp_cloud) | secret: no |
#              source: EXTERNAL (Meta WhatsApp Business)
#    format:   string id; no validation.
#    consumer: notification, notification-worker | since: main
WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
# >> WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN
#    required: optional-empty (required iff WHATSAPP_TRANSPORT=whatsapp_cloud) | secret: YES |
#              source: EXTERNAL (Meta)
#    format:   string; no validation. Sent as `Authorization: Bearer` to Meta's Graph API by the
#              platform-level `whatsapp_cloud` transport only. Each tenant's own token is NOT an
#              env variable: it is stored sealed per tenant (provider_accounts.access_token_enc).
#              The Graph API base (https://graph.facebook.com/v25.0) is not forwarded by compose,
#              so it cannot be changed from .env.
#    consumer: notification, notification-worker | since: main
WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# Optional Graph API overrides for notification, left out on purpose: they are not required and
# an empty value changes nothing. Add either line only to move off the code's default
# (`https://graph.facebook.com/v25.0`):
#   WHATSAPP_GRAPH_VERSION=v25.0        (replaces just the version segment)
#   WHATSAPP_API_BASE=https://graph.facebook.com/v25.0

# --- Engage (notification's campaign product) ----------------------------------
# Signs the unsubscribe link in every campaign mail (HMAC-SHA256). GENERATE FRESH — production
# refuses the `dev-only-` default, the same as every other signing secret here — and rotating it
# invalidates every unsubscribe link already sent. `python -c "import secrets;
# print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"` is as good a way to make one as any other.
# >> ENGAGE_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET
#    required: required | secret: YES | source: GENERATE on the box
#    format:   any string not starting `dev-only-`/`ci-only-` (HMAC-SHA256 key). NO length floor in
#              code; 64 hex chars is the recommendation. Do NOT leave it for later: in
#              production/staging/uat an empty or `dev-only-`/`ci-only-` value stops the
#              notification service from STARTING, which takes down every email (password
#              resets included), not just campaigns. Preflight's non-empty check runs first.
#              Rotating invalidates every unsubscribe link already sent.
#    generate: openssl rand -hex 32
#    consumer: notification, notification-worker (campaign send gate, link minting, unsubscribe
#              routes) | since: main (Engage, #34)
ENGAGE_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET=
# Where `/engage/unsubscribe/{token}` is actually reachable from the outside. The send gate
# (services/notification/app/services.py's `_check_public_base`) refuses to send a campaign
# outside local/test/ci while this is still `localhost`/`127.0.0.1` or plain `http://` — a
# campaign whose unsubscribe link nobody's mailbox can open is not sendable at all.
# >> ENGAGE_PUBLIC_API_BASE
#    required: required | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (the public API base, path ending /api/v1)
#    format:   absolute https URL WHOSE PATH ENDS IN /api/v1 (links are `<base>/engage/unsubscribe/<token>`
#              and the only public unsubscribe route is the prefix /api/v1/engage/unsubscribe/: a bare
#              origin passes preflight and the send gate but yields links Caddy does not proxy). Write it
#              lower case; a trailing slash is stripped. Not public, but accepted by both checks: `[::1]`
#              and `0.0.0.0`. preflight FAILs `localhost` and `127.0.0.1` case-sensitively; the send gate
#              lower-cases first, so `https://Localhost/api/v1` passes preflight and is refused at send.
#    consumer: notification, notification-worker | since: main (Engage, #34)
ENGAGE_PUBLIC_API_BASE=https://app.opsgrids.com/api/v1
# Optional key for the signed open/click tracking links in campaign mail (opt-in per campaign).
# Leave it unset and the key is derived from ENGAGE_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET, so tracking needs nothing
# new; set it to rotate tracking links independently of unsubscribe links. Rotating invalidates
# the tracking links already sent (opens stop being recorded, clicks on old mail answer 404).
# NOT YET FORWARDED: compose.prod.yml does not pass this variable to notification, so setting it
# here has no effect until every notification process that mints or verifies a token (the API and
# whichever process runs the campaign send driver) lists it: one with the key and one without
# would sign and verify with different keys, and every link would 404.
# >> ENGAGE_TRACKING_SECRET
#    required: optional-empty | secret: YES | source: GENERATE on the box
#    format:   at least 32 characters, not starting `dev-only-`/`ci-only-`. A shorter or placeholder
#              value is REFUSED: tracking is switched off (logged `engage.tracking.secret_too_short`
#              or `engage.tracking.secret_is_placeholder`), never quietly replaced by the derived key.
#    generate: openssl rand -hex 32
#    consumer: notification, notification-worker (tracking pixel and click routes, campaign send)
#              | since: E1
# ENGAGE_TRACKING_SECRET=

# --- database pool ------------------------------------------------------------
# Per process, request-serving pool only. ~31 processes (ten profiles: 18 plain APIs, 10
# APIs that also fold a former `*-worker`'s loops in, edge-relay, scheduler, ops-monitor) share
# one Postgres started with max_connections=400. The ten folded APIs (and operations/storefront/
# document, which fold a sweep) also open a second, two-connection bulkhead engine
# (create_bulkhead_engine) for that background work, never counted against this pool — worst
# case across all ten profiles is comfortably under 250 backends, well inside 400. This value
# used to read 5 here, silently overriding compose.prod.yml's own `${DB_POOL_SIZE:-3}` default —
# fixed to match it (the worker-folding follow-up to the memory-headroom slice, 2026-09-24).
# >> DB_POOL_SIZE
#    required: required | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (default is fine)
#    format:   integer, connections per process (request pool only; see the bulkhead note above).
#    consumer: every service (x-service-env) | since: main
DB_POOL_SIZE=3
# >> DB_MAX_OVERFLOW
#    required: required | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (default is fine)
#    format:   integer, per process.
#    consumer: every service (x-service-env) | since: main
DB_MAX_OVERFLOW=2

# --- platform -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Where the merchant-facing application lives. Used in emails and links. Slice 1 does not
# deploy any merchant SPA to this box — see the runbook's "known gaps".
# >> APP_BASE_URL
#    required: required | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (the merchant-facing app origin)
#    format:   absolute URL used in emails and links; no validator.
#    consumer: every service (x-service-env) | since: main
APP_BASE_URL=https://app.opsgrids.com
# Comma-separated. Custom domains add NOTHING to this list and must never be added to it: the
# buyer surface is same-origin through Caddy precisely so this stays a fixed set.
# >> CORS_ORIGINS
#    required: required | secret: no | source: CHOOSE
#    format:   comma-separated origins (parsed to a list); must stay a fixed set - custom domains
#              are never added here.
#    consumer: every service (x-service-env) | since: main
CORS_ORIGINS=https://app.opsgrids.com
# ADR-0003 addendum (SSO across app hosts without a domain-wide cookie): the refresh cookie is
# host-only on app.opsgrids.com, so every product SPA other than the dashboard calls
# `/api/v1/auth/refresh` and `/api/v1/auth/logout` cross-origin, straight at app-gateway, to
# reach it. This is the narrow, second CORS allowance for exactly those two routes — never
# widen CORS_ORIGINS itself for this, which would let every one of these origins call *every*
# route the gateway fronts, not just the two that need it. Storefront hosts
# (`*.stores.opsgrids.com`, custom merchant domains) must never be added: nothing there is
# meant to hold a platform session, and the anonymous buyer bundle never calls either route.
# >> AUTH_REFRESH_CORS_ORIGINS
#    required: required | secret: no | source: CHOOSE
#    format:   comma-separated origins (parsed to a list); every platform app host except
#              app.opsgrids.com itself (already covered by CORS_ORIGINS), every non-SPA host
#              (edge.opsgrids.com, api.opsgrids.com), and admin.opsgrids.com — the staff console
#              authenticates operators same-origin against admin-gateway, never this app-gateway
#              route, so it is never added here even though it is a TLS_PLATFORM_HOSTS entry.
#    consumer: app-gateway only — compose sets this on the app-gateway service specifically,
#              not through the shared x-service-env every other service reads
#    since: fix/session-refresh-sso
AUTH_REFRESH_CORS_ORIGINS=https://ops.opsgrids.com,https://worker.opsgrids.com,https://people.opsgrids.com,https://revenue.opsgrids.com,https://pulse.opsgrids.com,https://projects.opsgrids.com,https://support.opsgrids.com,https://marketing.opsgrids.com,https://finance.opsgrids.com,https://it.opsgrids.com,https://store.opsgrids.com,https://engage.opsgrids.com
# >> LOG_LEVEL
#    required: required | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (default is fine)
#    format:   an exact UPPER-CASE logging level name: DEBUG INFO WARNING WARN ERROR CRITICAL FATAL
#              NOTSET. The setting is an unconstrained string but anything else, lower case included
#              (`info`), raises `ValueError: Unknown level` and crashes every service at startup.
#    consumer: every service (x-service-env) | since: main
LOG_LEVEL=INFO
# How long a resolved store slug is cached in Redis: a newly published store can 404 for up to this
# long. It does NOT govern taking a custom domain off the air: that is a different setting,
# `store_host_cache_seconds` (default 300, services/storefront/app/config.py:65), which compose does not
# forward, so `.env` cannot change it.
# >> STORE_SLUG_CACHE_SECONDS
#    required: required | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (default is fine)
#    format:   integer seconds (pydantic int; no range check). TTL of the Redis slug -> store lookup
#              only; not the custom-domain takedown window (see above).
#    consumer: storefront | since: main
STORE_SLUG_CACHE_SECONDS=300

# --- backups ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Off-box object storage for `backup.sh`. A backup on this box's disk is not a backup.
# Any S3-compatible endpoint: the provider's object store, Backblaze B2, Cloudflare R2.
# >> BACKUP_S3_BUCKET
#    required: required | secret: no | source: EXTERNAL (an off-box S3-compatible bucket the owner
#              provides)
#    format:   existing bucket name at the provider. NOT the box-local MinIO.
#    consumer: backup.sh (host cron / `make edge-backup`) | since: main
BACKUP_S3_BUCKET=opsgrids-backups
# >> BACKUP_S3_PREFIX
#    required: required | secret: no | source: CHOOSE
#    format:   key prefix inside the bucket.
#    consumer: backup.sh | since: main
BACKUP_S3_PREFIX=edge/postgres
# Leave empty for AWS itself; set it for any other S3-compatible provider.
# >> BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT
#    required: optional-empty (empty = AWS S3) | secret: no | source: EXTERNAL (the provider's S3
#              endpoint URL)
#    format:   URL passed verbatim as `aws s3 --endpoint-url`.
#    consumer: backup.sh | since: main
BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT=
# >> BACKUP_S3_REGION
#    required: required | secret: no | source: EXTERNAL (the provider's region)
#    format:   region string, exported as AWS_DEFAULT_REGION.
#    consumer: backup.sh | since: main
BACKUP_S3_REGION=us-east-1
# GENERATE FRESH, scoped to write-only access on the backup bucket and nothing else. A
# credential that can also delete is a credential ransomware can use.
# >> BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID
#    required: optional-empty TOGETHER with the secret (both empty = WARN, exactly one set = FAIL) |
#              secret: YES | source: EXTERNAL (issued by the backup provider)
#    format:   whatever the provider issues; scope it to write-only on the bucket.
#    consumer: backup.sh | since: main
BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# >> BACKUP_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
#    required: optional-empty TOGETHER with the key id | secret: YES | source: EXTERNAL (issued with
#              the key id; `openssl rand -hex 32` only if your provider lets you choose)
#    format:   whatever the provider issues.
#    consumer: backup.sh | since: main
BACKUP_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# How many nightly dumps to keep on the box before the oldest is removed. The off-box copies
# are kept by the bucket's own lifecycle policy, which is set in the provider's console.
# >> BACKUP_LOCAL_KEEP
#    required: required | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (default is fine)
#    format:   positive integer >= 1, nightly dumps kept on the box. `0` deletes EVERY local dump,
#              the one just written included (`head -n -0`); a negative or non-numeric value fails
#              backup.sh after the upload (backup.sh:170-173).
#    consumer: backup.sh | since: main
BACKUP_LOCAL_KEEP=7

# --- object storage: document-service's public asset store (ADR-0009) ---------
# Backs the storefront console's image upload (`/api/v1/documents/assets`) and is exercised
# only when the `storefront` profile is enabled below — document and MinIO both sit behind it
# (compose.prod.yml). Generate real values regardless: preflight's "every key in .env.example
# must be set" rule does not special-case a profile that happens to be off, and a box that never
# sells storefront just never starts the two containers that would read these.
#
# GENERATE FRESH. MinIO's root credential. `minio-init` (compose.prod.yml) uses it once, at
# startup, to create the opsgrids-public bucket and its anonymous-read policy, and to create the
# scoped `document` user below. Nothing else ever authenticates as root. MinIO requires at least
# 8 characters for the password.
# >> MINIO_ROOT_USER
#    required: required (even if the storefront profile is off) | secret: YES | source: GENERATE on
#              the box
#    format:   no repo-enforced format, but compose embeds it (as the user part) in the `MC_HOST_og`
#              connection URL, so it must be URL-userinfo-safe: hex is.
#    generate: openssl rand -hex 16
#    consumer: minio, minio-init | since: release/backlog-complete (document service + MinIO,
#              ADR-0009)
MINIO_ROOT_USER=
# >> MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD
#    required: required (even if the storefront profile is off) | secret: YES | source: GENERATE on
#              the box
#    format:   >= 8 characters (preflight FAILs shorter); URL-userinfo-safe (hex).
#    generate: openssl rand -hex 32
#    consumer: minio, minio-init | since: release/backlog-complete (document service + MinIO,
#              ADR-0009)
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=
# GENERATE FRESH. The scoped credential `document` actually connects with — `minio-init` creates
# this user and attaches a policy granting only `s3:PutObject` and `s3:DeleteObject` on
# `S3_PUBLIC_BUCKET`'s objects (`document` never calls GetObject or ListBucket; see
# `services/document/app/storage.py`). A compromised or merely buggy `document` process can put
# and delete objects in this one bucket and nothing more — it cannot list the bucket, touch a
# second bucket, or rewrite this bucket's own anonymous-read policy, which the MinIO root
# credential would have handed it for free. MinIO requires at least 8 characters for the secret.
# >> DOCUMENT_S3_ACCESS_KEY
#    required: required (even if the storefront profile is off) | secret: YES | source: GENERATE on
#              the box
#    format:   a free-choice access key; minio-init CREATES a MinIO user with this name. Keep it
#              different from MINIO_ROOT_USER (MinIO/mc behaviour, provider-side, not verified in repo;
#              generating each value separately guarantees it). MinIO's own length limits are not
#              stated in the repo; hex-16 is the recommendation.
#    generate: openssl rand -hex 16
#    consumer: document, minio-init (and document-cron before the memory-headroom merge, 193b529) | since:
#              release/backlog-complete (document service + MinIO, ADR-0009)
DOCUMENT_S3_ACCESS_KEY=
# >> DOCUMENT_S3_SECRET_KEY
#    required: required (even if the storefront profile is off) | secret: YES | source: GENERATE on
#              the box
#    format:   >= 8 characters (preflight FAILs shorter).
#    generate: openssl rand -hex 32
#    consumer: document, minio-init (and document-cron before the memory-headroom merge, 193b529) | since:
#              release/backlog-complete (document service + MinIO, ADR-0009)
DOCUMENT_S3_SECRET_KEY=
# GENERATE FRESH. The orphan pass's own credential (ADR-0009 §9 addendum), held by no always-on
# container — only handed, by hand, to a one-off `docker compose run --rm --no-deps document
# ./bin/cron` (the purge sweep itself runs inside the `document` API without it, since the
# memory-headroom slice's fix round 2). `minio-init` creates this user and attaches a policy granting
# it `s3:ListBucket` on the bucket plus `s3:DeleteObject` on its objects, so the orphan pass
# can list what is actually in the bucket and remove what has no row. `document`'s own API
# container never receives these two values and never calls `ListBucket`
# (`services/document/app/storage.py`'s `ObjectStore` vs. `OrphanScanner`) — a compromised or
# merely buggy API process still cannot enumerate the bucket, which is the whole of ADR-0009
# §3's tenancy argument. Same 8-character minimum as the pair above.
# >> DOCUMENT_S3_ORPHAN_ACCESS_KEY
#    required: optional-empty in preflight; RECOMMENDED on any box with the storefront profile (see
#              format) | secret: YES | source: GENERATE on the box
#    format:   same shape as DOCUMENT_S3_ACCESS_KEY, and different from it. minio-init runs `mc
#              admin user add` with this pair unconditionally; what mc does with two empty strings
#              is not verifiable from the repo, so set both at first deploy.
#    generate: openssl rand -hex 16
#    consumer: minio-init (creates the list+delete user); document-cron before the memory-headroom
#              merge (193b529) | since: release/backlog-complete (document service + MinIO, ADR-0009)
DOCUMENT_S3_ORPHAN_ACCESS_KEY=
# >> DOCUMENT_S3_ORPHAN_SECRET_KEY
#    required: optional-empty in preflight; RECOMMENDED with the access key | secret: YES | source:
#              GENERATE on the box
#    format:   >= 8 characters (NOT length-checked by preflight).
#    generate: openssl rand -hex 32
#    consumer: minio-init; document-cron before the memory-headroom merge (193b529) | since:
#              release/backlog-complete (document service + MinIO, ADR-0009)
DOCUMENT_S3_ORPHAN_SECRET_KEY=
# Not a secret. How long a soft-deleted asset's object survives before the sweep removes it
# from the bucket (ADR-0009 §8). The committed default matches the ADR; raise or lower it here
# without touching code.
# >> ASSET_PURGE_RETENTION_DAYS
#    required: required | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (committed default matches ADR-0009 s8)
#    format:   integer days.
#    consumer: document (the in-API purge sweep since ce35c77); document-cron before | since:
#              release/backlog-complete (document service + MinIO, ADR-0009)
ASSET_PURGE_RETENTION_DAYS=30
# Not a secret. Off, and reporting-only even once on (ADR-0009 §9 addendum) — an automated,
# bucket-wide delete earns that much caution. Turn DOCUMENT_ORPHAN_SWEEP_ENABLED on only once
# DOCUMENT_S3_ORPHAN_ACCESS_KEY/_SECRET_KEY above are set; leave DOCUMENT_ORPHAN_SWEEP_DRY_RUN
# at true until a run or two of its logged "would delete" candidates looks right, then flip it
# to false to let the sweep actually call DeleteObject.
# >> DOCUMENT_ORPHAN_SWEEP_ENABLED
#    required: required | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (leave false until the orphan pair is set)
#    format:   true|false. Since the memory-headroom merge (ce35c77) NO container reads it (the
#              orphan pass runs only by hand); on 193b529 document-cron did.
#    consumer: document-cron on 193b529 only; no container since ce35c77 | since:
#              release/backlog-complete (document service + MinIO, ADR-0009)
DOCUMENT_ORPHAN_SWEEP_ENABLED=false
# >> DOCUMENT_ORPHAN_SWEEP_DRY_RUN
#    required: required | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (keep true until dry runs look right)
#    format:   true|false. Reporting-only while true.
#    consumer: document-cron on 193b529 only; no container since ce35c77 | since:
#              release/backlog-complete (document service + MinIO, ADR-0009)
DOCUMENT_ORPHAN_SWEEP_DRY_RUN=true
# Not a secret. Public-read in its entirety (ADR-0009 §2) — nothing that is not meant to be
# world-readable is ever written here. Created by `minio-init` on first deploy. Caddy's
# `/opsgrids-public/*` proxy matcher is driven by this same variable (`caddy/Caddyfile`), so
# renaming it here is a one-value change and not a second hardcoded path to remember — just
# redeploy after changing it, and rename it in `PUBLIC_ASSET_BASE_URL` below in the same edit.
# >> S3_PUBLIC_BUCKET
#    required: required | secret: no | source: CHOOSE (committed default is fine)
#    format:   bucket name; must be the last path segment of PUBLIC_ASSET_BASE_URL (preflight compares
#              the strings). No repo code validates the characters: the value is interpolated into a
#              Caddy path matcher, an S3 ARN and a URL path, so keep to lower-case letters, digits and
#              hyphens (S3/MinIO's own naming rules are provider-side, not verified in repo).
#    consumer: caddy (path matcher), minio-init (creates it), document | since:
#              release/backlog-complete (document service + MinIO, ADR-0009)
S3_PUBLIC_BUCKET=opsgrids-public
# Not a secret. What a stored `/a/{organization_id}/{asset_id}` reference resolves against
# (ADR-0009 §6): document returns it for the editor's own immediate preview, and it is what the
# storefront bundle's `VITE_ASSET_BASE_URL` must be built with so a buyer's browser resolves the
# same address. Caddy reverse-proxies `/opsgrids-public/*` straight to MinIO, but ONLY on
# EDGE_HOST's own site block (`caddy/Caddyfile`) — not on every host it serves, precisely so a
# malicious object cannot be served back as HTML on a platform hostname carrying the
# `.opsgrids.com`-scoped refresh cookie (MinIO honours `?response-content-type=` on an
# anonymous GetObject). So this must be `https://<EDGE_HOST>/opsgrids-public`, exact — the
# committed default matches this box's own EDGE_HOST above; change both together if you rename
# the box, and never point it at a different host Caddy serves, even one in TLS_PLATFORM_HOSTS.
# >> PUBLIC_ASSET_BASE_URL
#    required: required | secret: no | source: DERIVED from EDGE_HOST and S3_PUBLIC_BUCKET
#    format:   EXACTLY `https://<EDGE_HOST>/<S3_PUBLIC_BUCKET>`, no trailing slash - preflight
#              compares the strings and FAILs on any difference. Republish the buyer + storefront
#              bundles after changing it. publish-web.sh reads this line raw over ssh (first line; only
#              a CR and one quote pair are stripped, an inline ` # comment` is KEPT and baked into
#              VITE_ASSET_BASE_URL while preflight ignores it): write it bare, no quotes, no comment.
#    consumer: document; baked into the storefront bundle by publish-web.sh | since:
#              release/backlog-complete (document service + MinIO, ADR-0009)
PUBLIC_ASSET_BASE_URL=https://edge.opsgrids.com/opsgrids-public

# Optional, and deliberately COMMENTED OUT (an uncommented key here becomes required on every
# box, and preflight would refuse a deploy until it was filled in; this key is set by the owner on
# its own schedule and the map is built to run without it). The Google Maps JavaScript API key
# for the warehouse map in the Operations app (ops.opsgrids.com): the sites map, the warehouse
# location picker and the "show worker location" pin. Read by publish-web.sh and nothing else.
# It is NOT a server secret: Vite bakes it into the operations bundle at publish time, so every
# visitor's browser receives it, and the protection that counts is the restriction set on the key
# in the Google Cloud console (HTTP referrers limited to https://ops.opsgrids.com/*, the API list
# limited to what the app calls, and a budget / quota cap). Never put a server-side Google key,
# or any other secret, in this variable. Absent or empty = the bundle is published WITHOUT the
# map behind a loud warning (the sites page shows a table, the warehouse dialog takes coordinates
# by hand): nothing breaks. The worker app never gets it. Republish operations after changing it
# (`make edge-web APP=operations`); a change here does nothing to a bundle already published.
# >> VITE_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY  (COMMENTED ON PURPOSE: not part of the required key set)
#    required: optional; absent or empty = map disabled | secret: no (public by design; restrict
#              it in the Google Cloud console) | source: EXTERNAL (an API key from a Google Cloud
#              project; the owner creates it)
#    format:   letters, digits, `-` and `_`, at least 20 of them, written bare (no quotes, no
#              inline comment: publish-web.sh reads the last `VITE_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=` line
#              over ssh and drops a trailing ` # comment`, but a value that fails this shape is
#              treated as no key at all, with a warning that never prints it).
#    consumer: publish-web.sh (`APP=operations`) ONLY, baked into the bundle as
#              `import.meta.env.VITE_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY`; no container and no other app reads it |
#              since: warehouse map and storage put-away (docs/superpowers/specs/
#              2026-10-05-warehouse-map-putaway-design.md)
# VITE_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=

# --- observability ------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional. This platform ships no error tracking of its own; if a Sentry project exists,
# putting its DSN here is the cheapest thing that turns an unnoticed 500 into a notification.
# Leave it empty and the services simply do not report.
# >> SENTRY_DSN
#    required: optional-empty | secret: YES | source: EXTERNAL (a Sentry project, if one exists)
#    format:   Sentry DSN URL; empty = no error reporting. NOTE: inert in this release - the setting is
#              declared (platform_core/config.py `sentry_dsn`) but no code reads it and uv.lock has
#              no Sentry package, so filling it in changes nothing until an SDK is wired in.
#    consumer: every service (x-service-env) passes it through; nothing consumes it | since: main
SENTRY_DSN=

# --- ops-monitor (C10, R7, R37) -------------------------------------------------
# The box's own alerting — outbox lag, DLQ depth, API readiness, disk, backup freshness and
# certificate expiry, checked every 60s (RUNBOOK.md, "alerts and what each means"). Delivers by
# direct SMTP, reusing EMAIL_FROM and SMTP_* above (the box's own mailbox) — deliberately NOT
# through notification-service, which is one of the things being monitored. Leave OPS_ALERT_EMAIL
# empty and it still checks and still logs `ops.alert.firing` at error, it just pages nobody;
# preflight warns about this in capitals, the same as EMAIL_TRANSPORT=log above.
# >> OPS_ALERT_EMAIL
#    required: optional-empty (line must exist; empty = alerts logged, nobody paged) | secret: no |
#              source: CHOOSE (mailboxes a human reads)
#    format:   one address, or SEVERAL COMMA-SEPARATED (no spaces): the value goes verbatim into the
#              To header and smtplib derives the recipients from it (verified offline for `a,b`; NO repo
#              test sends to more than one address). `;`, spaces and the word `and` are NOT separators -
#              only the first address would receive mail, silently. Quote the value if it contains a
#              space (preflight `source`s this file as bash). The owner's stated recipients for this box
#              are info@opsgrids.com,zionna@opsgrids.com (the owner's instruction, not in the repo): set
#              them in checklist step 5.1, AFTER the SMTP login probe printed OK, because setting this
#              key is what makes ops-monitor start sending. It is empty here on purpose.
#    consumer: ops-monitor ONLY | since: release/backlog-complete (ops-monitor, merged at 50756f7)
OPS_ALERT_EMAIL=
# Comma-separated `name[:port]`. Which API containers get a `/health/ready` check, checked
# concurrently. Empty (the normal case) means "derive from COMPOSE_PROFILES above" — the
# always-on core (auth, account, product, app-gateway, catalog, subscription, billing,
# metering, payment, notification) plus each enabled profile's own containers
# (`platform_core.ops_monitor.config.expected_api_containers` — read its docstring for the full
# per-profile list). Set this explicitly only to narrow the computed list or add a name it does
# not know about yet; doing so REPLACES the computed list entirely, it does not add to it. A
# name that does not resolve is a REAL failure now (round 2 of C10's review found that Docker's
# embedded DNS drops a stopped/crashed/OOM-killed/restarting container exactly the way it drops
# one that was never deployed — there is no way to tell those apart, so both alert).
# >> OPS_MONITOR_API_CONTAINERS
#    required: optional-empty (line must exist; empty is the normal state) | secret: no | source:
#              DERIVED from COMPOSE_PROFILES when empty
#    format:   comma-separated `name[:port]`, each name matching ^[a-z_][a-z0-9_.-]*$. A non-empty
#              value REPLACES the derived list. The `:port` half is NOT validated at startup: a bad one
#              (`auth:80x`) aborts every monitoring cycle after the DLQ check (logged, and shown as
#              last_cycle_error on /status) so the api, disk, backup and cert checks never report.
#    consumer: ops-monitor ONLY | since: release/backlog-complete (ops-monitor, merged at 50756f7)
OPS_MONITOR_API_CONTAINERS=
# Comma-separated. Which consumer queues get a `{queue}.dlq` depth check, checked concurrently.
# Empty (the normal case) means "derive from COMPOSE_PROFILES above" — the always-on core
# (account.events, product.events, subscription.events, payment.events, notification.events,
# notification.dispatch) plus each enabled profile's own worker queue
# (`platform_core.ops_monitor.config.expected_dlq_queues`). Same replace-not-append rule as
# OPS_MONITOR_API_CONTAINERS above if set explicitly.
# >> OPS_MONITOR_DLQ_QUEUES
#    required: optional-empty (line must exist; empty is the normal state) | secret: no | source:
#              DERIVED from COMPOSE_PROFILES when empty
#    format:   comma-separated consumer queue names without `.dlq`, each matching
#              ^[a-z_][a-z0-9_.-]*$. A non-empty value REPLACES the derived list. A name that already ends
#              in `.dlq`, or is misspelt, is accepted, checked as `<x>.dlq.dlq`, and reports ok ("not
#              declared yet") forever: silently un-monitored. Leave it empty.
#    consumer: ops-monitor ONLY | since: release/backlog-complete (ops-monitor, merged at 50756f7)
OPS_MONITOR_DLQ_QUEUES=

# --- NOT keys of this file - do not add them ------------------------------------------------
# Adding an uncommented KEY= line above is what makes preflight require it, so the names below are
# described in prose only. Details and the exact commands are in docs/deploy/ENV_SOURCE_MATRIX.md
# (section 4 lists every one of them) and docs/deploy/ENV_SETUP_CHECKLIST.md.
#
# The auth service's legacy-plaintext MFA flag (merged into release/backlog-complete at 13c7181): NOT
#   a key of this file or of the box's `.env`, and it is deliberately not named here
#   (tests/infra/test_mfa_reseal.py forbids the name in this file and in compose). The deploy sets it
#   itself, on ONE one-off container (`remote.sh migrate` -> lib/mfa-reseal.sh), between `migrate` and
#   `up`, and once more, warn-only, after `health`; never on the running auth container. It skips when
#   the auth table does not exist yet or when no plaintext row remains (count 0, the steady state after
#   the first deploy); a count it cannot read aborts the deploy. Steady state:
#   unset. See docs/deploy/ENV_SOURCE_MATRIX.md, section 7.
# EDGE_STATE_DIR: process environment only (default /srv/opsgrids/state). Leave unset: remote.sh
#   does not read `.env`, so setting it here would move preflight's and compose's state directory
#   but not remote.sh's.
# EDGE_MEMINFO_FILE: a test seam for preflight's memory gate on a host without /proc/meminfo.
#   Never set it on the box.
# EDGE_RELAY_SCHEMAS (integration branch): a compose-only interpolation with a default. Leave unset.
#   (RUN_SWEEPS_IN_API and EDGE_UP_PARALLEL_LIMIT are shown, commented, next to EDGE_BUDGET_ACCEPTED_MB.)
# EDGE_REPO, HEALTH_TIMEOUT, FORCE_MIGRATE, FORCE_PULL, BACKUP_LOCAL_DIR: deploy-tooling process
#   environment, each with a default; docs/deploy/ENV_SOURCE_MATRIX.md, section 4. (ssh carries no
#   environment, so `make deploy` cannot set the ones remote.sh reads on the box.)
# PLACEHOLDER: a `make edge-web` argument (PLACEHOLDER=1), not a `.env` key.
# OPS_MONITOR_PORT: read only by `remote.sh status` from `.env` (default 9110); compose fixes the
#   container's port at 9110, so setting it would only misdirect the status probe. Leave unset.
# ROUTES_SHA: exported by remote.sh from routes.prod.json. Never set by hand.
